  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nFollowing warmer temperatures on Sunday, the week ahead looks pleasant, \nwith above normal temperatures increasing through the week. \nHigh pressure will reign through Friday as the center of the high \nmigrates from the central US to over the Great Lakes. Midlevel \nflow will remain southwesterly, providing WAA that will lead to \nabove normal temperatures through the week. A few shortwaves are \nexpected to move through and bring some cloud cover to the region, \nthough dry low levels will keep us free from any precipitation \nthrough much of the week. Couple of things to watch for are \n1) temperatures being a few degrees warmer than forecast during the\nday and cooler at night given the amount of dry air favorable for \nburgeoning diurnal ranges, and 2) potential for some fire weather \nconcerns given the dry weather and warming temperatures during \nany windier periods. Right now though, it appears being more \nunder the influence of the surface ridging that the stronger pressure\ngradient and winds will be favored to our west, until late week.     \n\nBeyond 7 days... \n\nRecurving typhoons in western Pacific often portend a central/eastern \nCONUS trough in about 6-14 days (buckling of jet orientation over \nthe north Pacific which can pump up the ridge into Alaska and the \nwestern CONUS in turning fostering an eastern trough), and probably \ncloser to 14 days in this case with Typhoon Champi recurving further \nwest towards China. Something to monitor for a pattern change to\ncolder and potentially a period of unsettled/wet weather. Latest \nCPC 8-14 day outlooks Oct 24-30 lean above normal on precipitation \nand likely above normal temperatures though again toward the end of\nthe period is when we may see the evolution toward more troughing and\ncolder conditions.",
